WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.317
Model size is often described by parameter count.

00:00:04.317 --> 00:00:15.903
Labels such as 3B, 7B, 8B, 14B, 32B, and 70B indicate billions of parameters, although architecture and implementation affect actual memory, quality, and speed.

00:00:15.903 --> 00:00:22.037
Smaller models usually load faster, need less memory, and generate more quickly.

00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:31.351
They can be excellent for classification, rewriting, extraction, short assistance, narrow domain workflows, and high-volume automation when evaluated carefully.

00:00:31.351 --> 00:00:40.666
Larger models may offer stronger reasoning, knowledge, instruction following, or difficult-task performance, but they require more memory and compute.

00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:47.709
They may also increase latency, power use, deployment cost, and the consequences of concurrency.

00:00:47.709 --> 00:00:50.662
Bigger is not always better.

00:00:50.662 --> 00:01:05.429
A large model running mostly on a slow CPU or constantly near memory limits may be less useful than a smaller model fully accelerated with room for context and other applications.

00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:08.383
Model family matters alongside size.

00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:17.697
Training data, architecture, tokenizer, prompt format, specialization, licensing, and alignment can make two similarly sized models behave very differently.

00:01:17.697 --> 00:01:21.559
Compare task results, not parameter labels alone.

00:01:21.559 --> 00:01:25.876
Architecture can also change compute and cache behavior.

00:01:25.876 --> 00:01:37.917
Two models with the same headline parameter count may need different memory, context overhead, prompt formats, or runtime support, so size categories remain planning approximations.

00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:40.870
Plan for the whole workload.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:50.639
A model that fits for one short prompt may fail with long context, vision components, multiple sessions, or large outputs.

00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:56.773
Keep memory headroom for cache, runtime, operating system, and recovery from bursts.
